  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/1/1d/Tales_to_Astonish_Vol_1_47.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Ant-Man and the Wasp are finishing up capturing Ramond Theis, \"an internationally wanted criminal\" who was attempting a diamond heist, targeting in particular, the priceless \"Star of Ghama.\"&nbsp; Afterward, the Indian owner, Nehradu, thanks them.&nbsp; He then goes on to inform them of a man from India named Ghazandi who can hypnotize both cobras and humans with his music, though it appears he is <i>afraid</i> to, \"for if he plays the wrong note, it will affect <i>him</i> also, and it will make Ghazandi lose his mystic power!\"\n\nOn the way home after the successful caper, Jan convinces Hank to go see a jazz concert afterward, but they soon see the star of the featured act -- a man named Trago, who is a petty crook and trumpet musician -- trying to make off with his manager's cash box.&nbsp; Ant-Man and the Wasp soon capture him.\n\nBut the manager of the jazz club decides not to press charges, instead electing to buy Trago a ticket on the first plane out of the country, but tells him that's as far as friendship goes -- \"Don't come back!\"&nbsp; And it so happens that the first plane out leaves for New Dehli [sic:&nbsp; should be \"Delhi\"], India.&nbsp; For Ant-Man and the Wasp, it seems like nothing more than another mundane case wrapped up.&nbsp; The two head out.\n\nTwo months later, near New Delhi, India (spelled correctly this time), we find Trago, who has of course tracked down Ghazandi.&nbsp; Trago is looking for the power Ghazandi has to charm snakes with his music.&nbsp; Ghazandi agrees to teach him.&nbsp; But Trago is impatient, wanting to hypnotize humans rather than just reptiles.&nbsp; Ghazandi warns of the dangers of doing this.\n\nThe narrative text then indicates that \"time passes\" and \"the months fly by,\" during which time Trago trains under the discipleship of Ghazandi.&nbsp; Later, Trago returns to the US, and during a gig in Connecticut, we learn that he holds the other musicians in his group under his spell, and he next mesmerizes the audience.&nbsp; Though he's easily able to take their valuables, he ultimately decides the small haul is not worth his bother and decides in the end that it was good first test of his new powers.\n\nDeciding he's ready for New York, he contacts a local TV station to broadcast his music to millions -- he'll soon hold an entire <i>city</i> under his spell!\n\n\"A few days later,\" Wasp hears a notice about \"Trago and his Magic Trumpet\" on a local radio station and calls Hank over to the radio.&nbsp; But when the music begins playing, they immediately realize they're being affected by its mesmerizing effect.\n\nAs they both shrink to ant-size, Hank calls out to Korr, a trusted ant, just before they fall victim to Trago's music.&nbsp; Korr is able to summon help, and before long, an army of ants arrive and carry away the now-helpless pair.\n\nA large portion of the city's populace is now under the mental control of Trago, and he orders his musician thralls to loot the banks where his music has been played, all while remembering who it was who thwarted him the last time he was in town.&nbsp; He musically orders that every reptile within earshot of his broadcasted performance begin the hunt for Ant-Man.&nbsp; He <i>must</i> be captured!\n\nAll the while, Korr and his friends carry the two comatose humans down into an ant-hill, well out of the reach of the hypnotic blare of Trago's trumpet.&nbsp; An enthralled garden snake attacks the anthill, killing faithful Korr in the process before it is finally stopped by the other ants.&nbsp; After ensuring that his cybernetic helmet (and Jan's) are able to block the hypnotic sounds, Ant-Man then hops on Foss, brother of Korr, and the two head off to the radio station to confront Trago, for Ant-Man has suddenly realized that Trago <i>must</i> have learned the art of hypnotism from the Indian man named Ghazandi that the diamond owner, Nehradu, told them about months earlier.\n\nHowever, Trago sees Hank and tries to smash him, but Hank jumps into the trumpet while his army of ants attacks Trago directly.&nbsp; Hank tampers with the trumpet, causing Trago to play the special notes (\"by chance\" the text says -- convenient) that sap away his power.&nbsp; Trago wakes up, as if from a trance.&nbsp; So does the rest of the city.\n\nSince no theft had actually occurred yet, and since Trago has no memory of what he did, they're content to let him do what he loves best -- play his trumpet for a living.&nbsp; Hank, though, is sad about his ant friend Korr.",
  "grade_one_liner": "A misfire built around Trago, whose \"magic trumpet\" is even sillier than it sounds.\nThankfully, the backup story shows up and does what Trago does <i>not</i>:&nbsp; <b>entertain</b>.",
  "subtitle": "Hypnosis by Horn; Stagnation by Story",
  "review_text": "<i>Ugh</i>, at first this seems like a middling tale, but it really descends into silliness, especially when an Indian caretaker (named Nehradu) of one of the most precious diamonds in the world expresses his gratitude in the most bizarre way imaginable, by randomly informing Ant-Man and the Wasp of a man (by the name of Ghazandi) from his home country of India who can hypnotize cobras (and humans) with his music.\n\nBut he goes out of his way to mention that Ghazandi specifically chooses <i>not</i> to use this ability, \"for if he plays the wrong note, it will affect <i>him</i> also, and it will make Ghazandi lose his mystic power!\"\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; text=It's such an oddly specific limitation that it practically <i>begs</i> to be exploited later.]\n\nNot only does Ant-Man then show us that he <i>names</i> his ants (e.g., \"Korr,\" the winged ant), but they happen to pass a jazz performance of \"Trago and his Magic Trumpet\" on the way home!\n[ASSET: center | TragoAndHisMagicTrumpet | width=67%]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | TheEndOfIt | width=16%] [ASSET: right | EvenTheMoonSmirks | width=29%] With comic‑book efficiency, the plot wastes no time:&nbsp; one thwarted cash box robbery attempt later has Trago and his Magic Trumpet on the first plane out of the country, which ironically places him in the city of New Delhi, India, the very location where the aforementioned Ghazandi lives.\n[BR]\n[BR] Ant-Man's exit line of \"Well, I guess that's the last we'll hear of the man with the magic trumpet!\" -- it's about the most blatant of clues which signals that after five pages of story, only the most ignorant of readers wouldn't have <i>immediately guessed</i> what was coming next.\n[BR]\n[BR] Even the <i>Moon</i> can't help but grin down at our two heroes (or was it really artist Don Heck expressing his disbelief at the lack of any subtlety on the part of the plot that caused him to draw the smirk on the Moon?&nbsp; And honestly, who could blame him?&nbsp; It's practically a reaction panel.)\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Groan!]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Can you spell \"Chekhov's gun?\")]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nThe story picks up \"two months later\" in the hut of Ghazandi, who of <i>course</i> has taken in starving trumpet player Trago.&nbsp; Page 7 even specifically says \"the months fly by\" -- what have Ant-Man and the Wasp been <i>doing</i> during all these months?&nbsp; (The story never even pretends to care.)\n\n[ASSET: left | RememberTrago | width=8%] The fact that the Indian man at the beginning mentions Ghazandi and the special notes that would sap his powers so that Hank would know to attack the trumpet (\"months\" later to boot) is just too convenient to be forgiven (even by early-'60s standards, where contrivances are practically a <i>feature</i>).\n\nAnt-Man very conveniently damages the \"magic trumpet\" in <i>just</i> the right way to cause it to emit the very notes required to remove Trago's powers (and his memory).\n[ASSET: center | ByChance | width=67%]\n\nAnd once again, the [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=day is saved].\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nI'm not sure why the title was \"Music to Scream By,\" as no one actually screamed, but this small nit aside, this story was just another in the ongoing and ever-insipid adventures of Ant-Man and the Wasp, easily justifying my own name for the book:&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Tales to Abolish], a name the book keeps <i>earning</i>, issue after relentlessly nauseating issue...\n\nThese people that show up in their lives, terrorize the city, and then are left to walk away scot free -- this is becoming a ridiculous trend in this book and not at all very realistic.&nbsp; I find it annoying.\n\nAnd naming the ants too?&nbsp; <i>Argh!</i>\n[BR] Just poor --  [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=truly, <b>why</b>?]\n\n[ASSET: right | GreaterLove | width=16%] Let's not forget the story's closing panels, which include a paraphrased quotation from the Biblical verse of John 15:13:&nbsp; \"Greater love hath no one than this, that he lay down his life for his friends!\"\n[BR]\n[BR] For the record, it's not the Scripture reference I object to -- it's the fact that it was applied to an <b>ant's</b> decision.&nbsp; Showing Hank depressed like this over the self-sacrificing action of a single <i>ant</i>?&nbsp; It's an emotional weight the story simply hasn't earned.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=I'm not buying it!]\n\n[DEVNOTE: Target...Earth!&nbsp; (put here for searching purposes)]\n\nIn the end, the book was saved from a bottom-tier grade, <i>not</i> because the Ant-Man/Wasp story was any good (it was anything <i>but</i>), but because of the back-up story called \"Target... Earth!\"&nbsp; In fact, this story provided such a laugh-out-loud moment -- one so strong that it instantly became a finalist for 1963's <b>Best Belly-Laugh Moment</b>.&nbsp; It was funny enough that I am electing to skip the description here and save that for the Year-End Summary.&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: And since I have already mentioned that I have read through the entire 1960s at this point, I still cannot think of a single panel from any back-up story that is funnier than the last one in this short, five-page story!&nbsp; <i>Hahahaha!</i>  In fact, it was so good that Stan re-used the ironic twist ending in one of his <i>Tales of the Watcher</i> features that was published almost a year later in <i>Tales of Suspense</i> 55.]\n\nThough admittedly trope-laden, it is so worth checking out that it <i>easily</i> added an entire point to the issue's otherwise <b>abysmal</b> final grade.",
  "side_note": [
    "[HANG0] * Ant-Man's name is lettered as \"Ant-Man\" (complete with hyphen) on story pages 1-6, but starting on page 7, it is rendered \"Antman\" (without the hyphen) in most, but not <i>all</i> places.&nbsp; (Page 11 has both within two panels of each other.)\n\n[HANG0] * Throughout the story, Jan and Hank use phrases like \"boyfriend\" and \"Honey\" that clearly show that the relationship is now progressing, even though Hank also continues to call Jan \"kid.\""
  ],
  "highlight": "Trago toots, Ant‑Man and the Wasp flail, and the backup story does all the heavy lifting."
}